Code that sclang compiles and a formatter is apt to spoil.

These are not forms of the grammar but traps for the rules: a character
whose second byte is a blank, a tab, a quote or a bracket; a comment or a
line break where one is seldom written; an operator hard against a number.
Most were written by a reviewer of this formatter to break it, and many
did. tests/lang11d.rs requires of each what it requires of the forms in
interpreted.forms: sclang compiles it, the grammar parses it, the formatter
takes it and is done in one pass, and sclang compiles what it wrote to the
same thing. The file is read as interpreted.forms is; the headings here are
only names. A tab in a form is a tab.

